---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { ORG_ID, absoluteUrl } from '@lib/seo'

// Technical reference page. The plain-language version for voters lives at
// /insights/who-can-see-what-you-believe/ and links here for detail.
//
// DISCLOSURE RULE for anyone editing this file: describe architectural
// properties and control categories, never implementation specifics. Do not
// name which tables or columns carry which protection, do not state software
// versions, host names, account identifiers, rate-limit thresholds, or which
// planned controls are not yet deployed. That last one matters most — stating
// that a specific defense is absent is an invitation. Certification status is
// a different question and is stated plainly below; that is safe to disclose
// and dishonest to omit.

const lastUpdated = 'August 1, 2026'

const pageSchema = {
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  '@id': `${absoluteUrl('/security')}#webpage`,
  name: 'Security at Voxivium',
  description:
    'How Voxivium protects voter data: identity separated from political belief by design, encryption, access control, retention limits, and the security standards the platform is engineered against.',
  url: absoluteUrl('/security'),
  isPartOf: { '@id': ORG_ID },
  dateModified: '2026-08-01',
  about: [
    { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Information security' },
    { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Data privacy' },
    { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Pseudonymization' },
  ],
}
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security at Voxivium — How Your Data Is Protected"
  description="How Voxivium separates your identity from your political beliefs at the database level, plus the encryption, access controls, and retention limits behind the platform."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Security', path: '/security' },
  ]}
  schema={[pageSchema]}
>
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">Security</p>
        <h1>How Voxivium protects your data.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Voxivium asks people to record what they believe about politics. That is sensitive
          information, and the platform is built on the assumption that it must never be traceable
          back to the individual who provided it. This page describes how that is enforced.
        </p>
        <p class="meta">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>

        <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
          <span class="toc-label">On this page</span>
          <a href="#separation">Identity separation</a>
          <a href="#encryption">Encryption</a>
          <a href="#access">Access control</a>
          <a href="#identity">Identity verification</a>
          <a href="#retention">Retention and deletion</a>
          <a href="#never">What we never do</a>
          <a href="#standards">Standards</a>
          <a href="#disclosure">Reporting an issue</a>
        </nav>

        <p class="aside">
          Prefer this without the technical detail? Read
          <a href="/insights/who-can-see-what-you-believe/">who can see what you believe</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="container" id="separation">
      <h2>Your identity is separated from your beliefs</h2>
      <p>
        This is the central design decision, and everything else is secondary to it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Voxivium has to know who you are. Verifying that each user is a real, registered U.S. voter
        scoped to an actual district is what makes the aggregate data meaningful, and it is what
        keeps bots and duplicate accounts from distorting it. Voxivium also has to know what you
        believe, because that is the product.
      </p>
      <p>
        It does not follow that those two facts should live together, and on Voxivium they do not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Personally identifiable information is held in a <strong>separate database</strong> from
        every record of political belief. The two are reachable only through different credentials,
        over a network path scoped so that only the application service can reach the sensitive
        side at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Belief records do not reference you. They reference an <strong>opaque pseudonymous
        identifier</strong> that carries no personal meaning and cannot be reversed into an
        identity by inspection. The direct identifiers that once linked these records were
        <strong>removed from the belief database entirely</strong> rather than merely hidden from
        queries. A copy of the belief database, taken on its own, does not contain the information
        needed to attribute a single position to a single person.
      </p>
      <p>
        Only one narrow service layer is capable of resolving a pseudonymous identifier back to a
        person, and it is the only path through which sensitive records are read or written.
        Application code elsewhere in the system has no route to that data.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          The practical consequence: the aggregate reporting that politicians, media organizations,
          and research partners pay for is computed entirely from the side of the system that does
          not know who anyone is.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container" id="encryption">
      <h2>Encryption</h2>
      <p>
        All traffic between your device and Voxivium is encrypted in transit using current TLS.
        Database connections are configured to refuse unencrypted sessions, so an internal service
        cannot accidentally open one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stored data is encrypted at rest, and automated backups are encrypted with it. Backups are
        retained on a defined schedule and are subject to the same access restrictions as the
        primary systems.
      </p>
      <p>
        Credentials, API keys, and service secrets are held in a managed secrets store with access
        scoped per service. None are committed to source control, and the repositories are
        configured so that they cannot be.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container" id="access">
      <h2>Access control and auditing</h2>
      <p>
        Access follows least privilege. Each service authenticates with its own credentials and
        holds only the permissions its function requires. The credentials used to reach sensitive
        data are distinct from those used for everything else, and are not interchangeable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Administrative operations require an explicit privileged claim rather than a role a normal
        account can acquire, and are additionally gated on session freshness, so a long-lived
        session cannot be used to perform a sensitive action without recent re-authentication.
      </p>
      <p>
        Privileged and out-of-band administrative operations authenticate against the cloud
        provider's own identity system rather than the application's, which means they cannot be
        reached with a stolen application credential.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every administrative action writes an audit record identifying the actor, the action, the
        records affected, and a before-and-after diff of what changed. Audit writes occur in the
        same transaction as the change itself, so an action cannot succeed without being recorded.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container" id="identity">
      <h2>Identity verification</h2>
      <p>
        Voxivium does not build its own document-and-liveness verification. That work is performed
        by <a href="https://didit.me/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Didit</a>, a specialist
        identity-verification provider, and Voxivium receives the verification outcome.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is deliberate. Document verification is a domain where a specialist with completed
        third-party audits is meaningfully safer than an in-house implementation. Didit holds SOC 2
        Type&nbsp;I, ISO/IEC 27001, ISO/IEC 27017, and ISO/IEC 27018 certifications, operates under
        a GDPR data processing agreement, and carries iBeta Level&nbsp;1 presentation-attack
        detection.
      </p>
      <p>
        Callbacks from the verification provider are authenticated with an HMAC signature over the
        message body and rejected outside a short freshness window, so a replayed or forged
        callback cannot mark an account as verified.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container" id="retention">
      <h2>Data minimization, retention, and deletion</h2>
      <p>
        Voxivium collects what verification and district matching require, and does not collect
        contact lists, device address books, browsing history, or location beyond the district
        resolution the product needs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Account deletion is available from account settings and runs in two stages.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Immediately.</strong> Personal fields are anonymized, the account is signed out,
          and your positions are removed from every district and national aggregate. Not filtered
          out at query time — the records are physically moved out of the tables that aggregate
          reporting reads, so no future query can reach them by oversight.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>After the retention window.</strong> A scheduled job permanently removes the
          remaining personal record and your belief and stance records from both databases,
          along with cached report cards and history.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The two-stage design exists so that an accidental deletion is survivable for a limited
        period while the visible effect is immediate. The full policy, including the categories of
        data collected and the legal bases for retention, is in the
        <a href="/legal/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container" id="never">
      <h2>What Voxivium does not do</h2>
      <ul class="commitments">
        <li>
          <strong>Individual data is never sold.</strong> Not to politicians, not to campaigns, not
          to media organizations, not to AI labs, not to anyone. The commercial product is
          aggregated, anonymized sentiment.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No customer ever sees an individual position.</strong> A politician sees that a
          share of verified constituents in their district holds a view. They cannot see who, and
          the system does not have a mechanism that would let them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No campaign targeting.</strong> Voxivium does not provide lists, segments, or
          any capability for reaching identified individuals based on their stated beliefs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No behavioral advertising and no tracking pixels.</strong> This website runs no
          third-party advertising or fingerprinting scripts.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="container" id="standards">
      <h2>The standards Voxivium is engineered against</h2>
      <p>
        Voxivium's architecture is built against established information security and privacy
        frameworks. Each is linked below with the part of the platform it informs.
      </p>

      <dl class="standards">
        <dt>
          <a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/27018" rel="noopener" target="_blank"
            >ISO/IEC 27018</a
          >
          <span class="std-sub">Protection of PII in public clouds</span>
        </dt>
        <dd>
          The framework closest to Voxivium's core problem. Its guidance on de-identification,
          purpose limitation, and restricting the ability to re-link data to an individual is what
          the identity-separation architecture above is designed to satisfy.
        </dd>

        <dt>
          <a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/27001" rel="noopener" target="_blank"
            >ISO/IEC 27001</a
          >
          <span class="std-sub">Information security management systems</span>
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Informs how security is managed rather than merely implemented: risk assessment, access
          control policy, cryptographic controls, logging, supplier relationships, and incident
          handling.
        </dd>

        <dt>
          <a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/27017" rel="noopener" target="_blank"
            >ISO/IEC 27017</a
          >
          <span class="std-sub">Security controls for cloud services</span>
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Addresses the division of responsibility between a cloud provider and its customer,
          separation in virtualized environments, and administrative-operation monitoring. Voxivium
          runs entirely on managed cloud infrastructure, which makes this allocation explicit
          rather than assumed.
        </dd>

        <dt>
          <a
            href="https://www.aicpa-cima.com/topic/audit-assurance/audit-and-assurance-greater-than-soc-2"
            rel="noopener"
            target="_blank">SOC 2</a
          >
          <span class="std-sub">AICPA Trust Services Criteria</span>
        </dt>
        <dd>
          The Security, Confidentiality, and Privacy criteria are the ones that bear on Voxivium,
          and they map onto the access control, audit logging, encryption, and retention practices
          described above. Voxivium requires SOC 2 attestation of the vendors that touch the most
          sensitive part of the flow.
        </dd>
      </dl>

      <div class="callout callout--status">
        <p>
          <strong>On certification status.</strong> Voxivium has not yet completed formal
          third-party audit against these frameworks. The architecture is built against them, and
          the identity-verification provider that handles document data is independently certified
          against several of them. When Voxivium completes its own audits, the results will be
          published here.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container" id="disclosure">
      <h2>Reporting a security issue</h2>
      <p>
        If you believe you have found a vulnerability, please report it to
        <a href="mailto:privacy@voxivium.com">privacy@voxivium.com</a>. Include enough detail to
        reproduce the issue. You will get an acknowledgment, and we will keep you informed as it is
        resolved.
      </p>
      <p>
        Please give us a reasonable opportunity to fix an issue before disclosing it publicly. We
        will not pursue legal action against researchers who investigate and report in good faith,
        act within the scope of their own accounts, avoid degrading the service, and do not access,
        modify, or retain other people's data.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">
        Machine-readable contact details are published at
        <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">/.well-known/security.txt</a> per
        <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9116.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank"
          >RFC 9116</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container">
      <div class="post-cta">
        <p>
          Voxivium is free for voters, and always will be. Every account is a verified U.S. voter,
          and no individual's positions are ever exposed.
        </p>
        <a class="cta" href="/#voters">Get early access</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .container {
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding-inline: 1.5rem;
  }

  .page-hero {
    padding: clamp(3.5rem, 9vh, 5.5rem) 0 1rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--color-brand-text3);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.6rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.15;
    color: var(--color-brand-text1);
    margin: 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .lead {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-brand-text3);
  }

  .meta {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-brand-text4);
  }

  .toc {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem 1.15rem;
    padding-block: 1rem;
    border-block: 1px solid var(--color-brand-el1);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .toc-label {
    color: var(--color-brand-text4);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .toc a {
    color: var(--color-brand-text3);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .toc a:hover {
    color: var(--color-brand-primary);
  }

  .aside {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-brand-text3);
  }

  section {
    padding-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--color-brand-text1);
    margin: 0 0 0.875rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-brand-text2);
  }

  strong {
    color: var(--color-brand-text1);
    font-weight: 640;
  }

  a {
    color: var(--color-brand-primary);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* Prose links must not rely on color alone to be distinguishable (WCAG 1.4.1).
     Scoped to body copy so the TOC chips and the CTA button keep their own
     treatment. */
  section a:not(.cta),
  .aside a {
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-brand-primary), transparent 60%);
  }

  section a:not(.cta):hover,
  .aside a:hover {
    border-bottom-color: var(--color-brand-primary);
  }

  ul {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-brand-text2);
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .commitments {
    list-style: none;
    padding-left: 0;
  }

  .commitments li {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  .commitments li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.65em;
    width: 0.5rem;
    height: 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--color-brand-primary);
  }

  .callout {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-brand-primary);
    background: var(--color-brand-surface);
    border-radius: 0 0.5rem 0.5rem 0;
  }

  .callout p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .callout--status {
    border-left-color: var(--color-brand-el3);
  }

  .standards {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }

  .standards dt {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    color: var(--color-brand-text1);
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .standards dt:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .std-sub {
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-brand-text4);
  }

  .standards dd {
    margin: 0.375rem 0 0;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-brand-text2);
  }

  .post-cta {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1.75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-brand-el1);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    background: var(--color-brand-surface);
  }

  .post-cta p {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }

  .cta {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--color-brand-primary);
    color: var(--color-brand-on-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 0.7rem 1.35rem;
    border-radius: 0.375rem;
  }

  .cta:hover {
    opacity: 0.9;
  }

  article {
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }
</style>
